            <div class="bg-white dark:bg-gray-800 rounded-xl shadow-sm border-2 border-gray-900 dark:border-gray-700 p-8">
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-white mb-4">How does this SIP calculator work?</h2>
                <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
                    The calculator takes three inputs: the amount you invest every month, the annual return you expect,
                    and the number of years. It converts the annual rate into a monthly rate (12% a year becomes 1% a
                    month), counts the total number of instalments, and assumes each instalment is invested at the start
                    of its month and grows at that same monthly rate until the end of the period.
                </p>
                <div class="bg-indigo-50 dark:bg-gray-700 p-4 rounded-lg border-l-4 border-indigo-500">
                    <p class="text-sm text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 font-mono">
                        FV = P x (((1 + i)^n - 1) / i) x (1 + i)
                    </p>
                    <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400 mt-2">
                        FV = projected value, P = monthly instalment, i = annual rate divided by 12 (as a decimal), n = years multiplied by 12
                    </p>
                </div>
                <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4 mt-4">
                    Worked example with the default inputs: ₹5,000 a month for 10 years at 12% gives i = 0.01 and n = 120.
                    The formula produces ₹11,61,695. You would have invested ₹6,00,000, so the projected gain is ₹5,61,695.
                </p>
                <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-200 mb-3 mt-6">The 12% is a nominal rate, and that matters</h3>
                <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
                    Because the annual rate is split into twelve equal monthly rates and compounded, "12% a year" in this
                    calculator is really 1% a month, which works out to an effective 12.68% a year. The same conversion
                    turns 10% into 10.47% and 8% into 8.30%. Fund factsheets quote annualised returns (CAGR), so a
                    fund that reports 12% CAGR corresponds to a slightly lower number in this calculator. The difference is
                    small over a few years but noticeable over twenty.
                </p>
                <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-200 mb-3 mt-6">What the calculator assumes</h3>
                <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4 space-y-1">
                    <li>The same return every single month. Real markets rise and fall, sometimes sharply.</li>
                    <li>No missed or delayed instalments and no change in the monthly amount.</li>
                    <li>Returns are net of the scheme's expense ratio, which is how fund NAVs are already reported.</li>
                    <li>No exit load, no capital gains tax and no inflation adjustment. The taxation section below covers tax.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

